---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout sideBarActiveItemID="work" title="Work | Agueda Schwartz">
  <div class="mb-16 mt-5">
    <div class="text-3xl font-bold mb-1">AI-Powered Entrepreneurship Platform</div>
    <div class="text-sm opacity-60 mb-4">WGU Labs | Aug 2025 to Present | Product Lead</div>

    <div class="prose max-w-none">
      <p>
        A 0-to-1 product exploring whether AI-enhanced delivery can bring a proven entrepreneurship methodology to people underserved by traditional accelerators and VCs.
      </p>
      <p>
        I own this end-to-end: strategy, research, prototyping, and day-to-day execution, with support from a user researcher and Claude Code as my primary working tool.
      </p>

      <h3>What I'm Doing</h3>
      <p>
        The project started stalled and ambiguous: no clear direction, no owner, multiple stakeholders waiting. I took it from there through a full product lifecycle.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Led strategic research combining primary data, landscape analysis, and partner methodology analysis (three sources that pointed in different directions)</li>
        <li>Synthesized conflicting data into a phased strategy that got executive buy-in</li>
        <li>Designed an AI behavior framework that defined not just what the AI should do, but what it explicitly should not</li>
        <li>Built and iterated prototypes, testing with real users to surface insights design reviews would have missed</li>
        <li>Applied my own innovation framework to the project mid-stream and found I'd over-invested in research and under-invested in user contact. Course-corrected within weeks.</li>
        <li>Ran a market validation sprint: persona-based landing pages, paid acquisition, data capture, and a go/pivot/kill framework</li>
        <li>Designed the business model and go-to-market strategy, including a dual B2C + B2B approach with viability experiments and kill criteria</li>
        <li>Made the pivot call based on evidence, not momentum. Now building from live cohort data.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Skills Demonstrated</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>0-to-1 product development:</strong> from no direction and no owner to a live product with real users</li>
        <li><strong>AI product design:</strong> defining AI behavior, boundaries, and interaction models</li>
        <li><strong>User research and discovery:</strong> interviews, persona development, usability testing, and synthesizing findings into product decisions</li>
        <li><strong>Decision-making under ambiguity:</strong> making a pivot call based on evidence when momentum pointed the other way</li>
        <li><strong>Business design:</strong> business model development, go-to-market strategy, and viability testing</li>
        <li><strong>Market validation:</strong> designing and executing a test that produces a real go/pivot/kill decision</li>
        <li><strong>Stakeholder alignment:</strong> achieving buy-in across conflicting priorities without formal authority</li>
        <li><strong>Solo execution:</strong> owning strategy through delivery as the only full-time PM</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <div class="mb-16">
    <div class="text-3xl font-bold mb-1">Innovation Framework for Product Development</div>
    <div class="text-sm opacity-60 mb-4">WGU Labs | Feb–Apr 2026 | Self-directed</div>

    <div class="prose max-w-none">
      <p>
        I was six months into a 0-to-1 product and realized I didn't have an operating model for how the work should move. I had good research, a working prototype, and a clear hypothesis structure, but no framework for when to stop analyzing and start testing, or how to make a kill decision if the evidence pointed that way.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I built one. A four-phase innovation loop (Sense, Shape, Test, Scale) with 4-8 week time-boxes, explicit stage gates, and go/pivot/kill criteria at each gate. Research gets deployed throughout the loop instead of front-loaded before anything else can happen.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I applied it to my own project. The assessment was honest and not flattering: I'd done thorough Sense and Shape work but had zero conversations with the target population. I was measuring output, not learning. The framework told me to get in front of users immediately, and I did. Within weeks the project went from zero user contact to a market test, an evidence-based pivot, and a build plan informed by real behavior.
      </p>

      <h3>What I Did</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Designed the innovation framework for my own use (not assigned)</li>
        <li>Applied it to my active project and documented the honest gaps</li>
        <li>Used the self-assessment to drive a course correction that produced real results</li>
        <li>Framework includes phase definitions, time-boxes, stage-gate criteria, role clarity, and weekly operating rhythm</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <div class="mb-10">
    <div class="text-2xl w-full font-bold mb-6">Also working on</div>

    <div class="mb-8">
      <div class="text-xl font-bold mb-1">AI Adoption Strategy</div>
      <div class="text-sm opacity-60 mb-2">WGU Labs | 2025 to Present | Collaborating with Ops</div>
      <div class="prose max-w-none">
        <p>
          Partnering with the operations team on how the organization adopts AI. My role is strategy and modeling. I run Claude Code daily across real product work, then translate what I learn into training, playbooks, and demos that help others adopt with confidence. I facilitated the team's first AI upskilling workshop and demonstrated the full PM architecture to leadership, which opened broader conversations about adoption across the org.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-8 mb-4 opacity-75 text-sm italic">
    Active work. Details kept high-level to protect proprietary information. Full artifacts available upon request.
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
